INVALID. Do not read a score off this run.

It was a ten-pair plumbing check of the acceptance setup: frozen directories, the
acceptance opening half, two workers pinned to their own physical cores. Sixteen minutes
in, while it was still drawing players from `snapshots/run1-baseline`, that directory was
deleted and rewritten so that all three agent directories would share byte-identical
source. The only textual difference was a comment in search.py and the engine's behaviour
is unchanged, but the run spans two directory states, and a run that does that is not
evidence. It is kept, marked, and not counted. It was stopped rather than finished.

What it did establish, and what is safe to take from it: the harness starts frozen
directories correctly, `--pin` gave the workers cores 0 and 1 as intended, the 35-cluster
acceptance opening file loads, and one pair of games at 20000ms + 200ms takes about seven
minutes of one worker. Two pairs finished cleanly with no agent failure.

The lesson is the one the previous handoff already wrote down: never touch a directory a
match is drawing players from. Everything after this point freezes its directories before
the match starts and does not touch them again.
